# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: vl-catch-all-apply-optional-arglist — BricsCAD accepts (vl-catch-all-apply 'f) with no argument list, clautolisp requires two arguments
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: divergence
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-vl-catch-all-apply-optional-arglist

* Context

Found 2026-08-16 while verifying =macroman-encoding-name= end to end:
once the encoding fault was out of the way, BricsCAD V26's own shipped
=vle-extension.lsp= got further and then failed on ARITY.

The call is the vendor's, not ours —
=/Applications/BricsCAD V26.app/Contents/MacOS/vle-extension.lsp=,
offset 4452:

#+begin_example
(vl-catch-all-apply 'vl-load-com)
(setq *hasAX* (and vlax-get-acad-object))
#+end_example

BricsCAD loads this file at startup, so BricsCAD necessarily accepts a
one-argument =vl-catch-all-apply=.

* Problem

clautolisp requires both arguments in every dialect:

#+begin_example
$ clautolisp --no-init --dialect bricscad -x '(progn (setq *AUTOLISP-FILE-ENCODING* "mac-roman")
    (load "/Applications/BricsCAD V26.app/Contents/MacOS/vle-extension.lsp"))'
clautolisp: runtime error: BUILTIN-ERROR: AutoLISP builtin
  VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY signaled an error: invalid number of arguments: 1
  in SUBR VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY: (VL-LOAD-COM)
#+end_example

So no ALPM system that references the installed BricsCAD library can be
loaded under =--dialect bricscad=: the vendor's own file is rejected by
the dialect that claims to emulate its engine.

The builtin is =BUILTIN-VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY=
(=autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp=, =REQUIRE-PROPER-LIST= on the
second argument at line 669) and is registered as a fixed-arity SUBR.

* Proposal

Make the argument list optional — =(vl-catch-all-apply FUNCTION
[ARG-LIST])=, defaulting to =nil=, i.e. call FUNCTION with no arguments
— and decide the dialect scope. Two questions to settle before coding,
one of which needs a probe:

1. *Which dialects?* =bricscad-v26= is established by the vendor file
   above. Whether AutoCAD accepts the one-argument form is NOT known:
   nothing here has probed it. Do not guess — add it to the probe suite
   (=probes/sources/=) and let the answer decide between "bricscad +
   lax + clautolisp" and "every dialect".
2. *Does =--strict= keep rejecting it?* Consistent with the treatment
   of other vendor-specific tolerances, =--strict= should stay strict
   and emit the usual divergence diagnostic rather than silently
   accepting a form only one engine takes.

* Acceptance criteria

1. [ ] =(vl-catch-all-apply 'f)= behaves as =(vl-catch-all-apply 'f nil)=
   in whichever dialects the probe establishes.
2. [ ] A probe records the AutoCAD answer, so the dialect scope rests on
   a measurement rather than an assumption.
3. [ ] =(load ".../BricsCAD V26.app/Contents/MacOS/vle-extension.lsp")=
   completes under =--dialect bricscad= with
   =*AUTOLISP-FILE-ENCODING*= set to ="mac-roman"=; regression test
   pinned on the ARITY, not on that specific vendor file (it is not
   redistributable and is absent on non-macOS hosts).
4. [ ] Spec entry for =vl-catch-all-apply= updated with the optional
   argument and the per-vendor divergence.
